Well, I have been misled by the MSC 5.1 Linker's /Exepack (/e) option.
After spending the weekend on it, it is apparent that it is a BAD thing
to use.  /e will sort of archive the executable file you make.  This
has the benifit of reducing the amount of disk space the file takes up.
The amount of free memory required to load the program actually INCREASES,
as you not only need the space originally required for your code but
also some extra to load the routine which will unarchive the /e'd executable.
By using the EXEMOD program, I found that specifying /e reduced the size
of the executable by about 50K, but increased the memory required to load the
program by 20K.  I removed the /e from my make file, and had enough free
space to enable the polyself option.
Here's the current staus of my compilation:
Size of executable: 613K
Free mem needed to load it: 567K
Free mem needed to load and still have enough ram for monsters: 590K
Options enabled: spells,polyself,thrones,hard,fountains,medusa,army,theology,
altars,seduce,stronghold,exp_on,botl,msdos,dgk,msdoscolor,termcap,wizard.
